WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:17.876
The next morning the rain poured down.

2
00:00:17.876 --> 00:00:22.863
When Mary looked out her window, the moor was almost hidden by gray mist.

3
00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:25.603
There would be no going out today.

4
00:00:25.603 --> 00:00:31.534
"What do you do in your cottage when it rains like this?" she asked Martha.

5
00:00:31.534 --> 00:00:35.553
"Try to stay out of each other's way," said Martha.

6
00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:41.470
"Dickon likes the wet. He says he sees things he doesn't see on fair days.

7
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:47.007
He once found a little fox cub half-drowned in a hole after its mother died.

8
00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:51.080
He brought it home, tucked under his shirt to keep warm."

9
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.873
Mary had grown to like Martha's stories.

10
00:00:54.873 --> 00:01:01.842
She especially liked the ones about Martha's mother, who seemed wise and kind, and her brother Dickon.

11
00:01:01.842 --> 00:01:06.048
"He found a crow another time," Martha went on.

12
00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:09.475
"He brought that one home too and tamed it.

13
00:01:09.475 --> 00:01:13.875
The crow's name is Soot because it's so black."

14
00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:18.470
"If I had a crow or a fox cub, I could play with it," said Mary.

15
00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.772
"But I have nothing."

16
00:01:20.772 --> 00:01:24.764
Martha looked puzzled. "Can you knit or sew?"

17
00:01:24.764 --> 00:01:26.389
"No."

18
00:01:26.389 --> 00:01:28.818
"Can you read?" asked Martha.

19
00:01:28.818 --> 00:01:30.812
"Yes."

20
00:01:30.812 --> 00:01:33.596
"Then why don't you read something?"

21
00:01:33.596 --> 00:01:36.104
"I don't have any books."

22
00:01:36.104 --> 00:01:43.802
"That's a pity. If Mrs. Medlock would let you go into the library, you'd have thousands of books to read."

23
00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.983
Mary did not care about the library.

24
00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:51.857
But hearing about it reminded her of the many rooms in the house.

25
00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:55.602
Were there really a hundred? Were they really all locked?

26
00:01:55.602 --> 00:02:00.655
Maybe she could count them. That would give her something to do.

27
00:02:00.655 --> 00:02:05.398
After Martha finished her work, Mary waited a few minutes.

28
00:02:05.398 --> 00:02:10.218
Then she opened her door and set off down the long corridor.

29
00:02:10.218 --> 00:02:17.987
It branched into other corridors, which led her up flights of steps that led to more steps.

30
00:02:17.987 --> 00:02:23.146
There were doors and more doors, and many portraits on the walls.

31
00:02:23.146 --> 00:02:28.612
The men and women wore grand costumes made of satin and velvet.

32
00:02:28.612 --> 00:02:33.392
She came upon a portrait of a plain girl rather like herself.

33
00:02:33.392 --> 00:02:39.095
"Where do you live now?" Mary asked the girl. "I wish you were here."

34
00:02:39.095 --> 00:02:43.206
It took a while before she thought to try opening a door.

35
00:02:43.206 --> 00:02:47.213
She was almost frightened when she felt the handle turn.

36
00:02:47.213 --> 00:02:54.659
She stepped into what looked like a bedroom, and on the wall was another portrait of the same little girl.

37
00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:57.272
Mary opened more doors.

38
00:02:57.272 --> 00:03:01.700
She opened so many that she grew tired of opening them.

39
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.399
There could have been a hundred, though she hadn't counted them.

40
00:03:05.399 --> 00:03:12.581
In one room, which looked like a lady's sitting room, she found a cabinet full of ivory elephants.

41
00:03:12.581 --> 00:03:15.930
She played with them until she became bored.

42
00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:20.394
Mary was shutting the cabinet door when she heard a rustling sound.

43
00:03:20.394 --> 00:03:26.531
On the sofa she spotted a family of mice living in a velvet cushion.

44
00:03:26.531 --> 00:03:33.978
"If they weren't so frightened, I would take them back with me," said Mary, who was ready to return to her room.

45
00:03:33.978 --> 00:03:37.400
She went out into the corridor again.

46
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.672
Two or three times she lost her way by turning down the wrong corridor.

47
00:03:42.672 --> 00:03:49.289
At last she reached her own floor, although she did not know exactly where she was.

48
00:03:49.289 --> 00:03:52.523
"I believe I've taken a wrong turn again."

49
00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:57.499
She was standing at the end of a short corridor with tapestries on the wall.

50
00:03:57.499 --> 00:04:02.174
"I don't know which way to go. How still everything is!"

51
00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:06.514
Suddenly the stillness was broken by a strange sound.

52
00:04:06.514 --> 00:04:11.687
It was a cry, but not quite like the one she'd heard before.

53
00:04:11.687 --> 00:04:15.071
"That sounds closer than it was last night."

54
00:04:15.071 --> 00:04:20.676
Mary's heart was beating faster. "And it is someone crying."

55
00:04:20.676 --> 00:04:27.542
She accidentally touched the tapestry near her and then sprang back, feeling quite startled.

56
00:04:27.542 --> 00:04:33.481
The tapestry covered a door, which swung open and revealed another corridor.

57
00:04:33.481 --> 00:04:40.723
Mrs. Medlock was coming down it with a bunch of keys in her hands and a very angry look on her face.

58
00:04:40.723 --> 00:04:46.936
"What are you doing here?" Mrs. Medlock took Mary by the arm and pulled her away.

59
00:04:46.936 --> 00:04:50.981
"What did I tell you about wandering through the house?"

60
00:04:50.981 --> 00:04:55.752
"I went the wrong way, and then I heard someone crying."

61
00:04:55.752 --> 00:05:00.220
"You heard nothing of the sort," said the housekeeper.

62
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.927
"You come along back to your own rooms."

63
00:05:03.927 --> 00:05:11.752
And she half-pushed and half-pulled Mary up one corridor and down another until they were standing in Mary's rooms.

64
00:05:11.752 --> 00:05:14.889
"Now," said Mrs. Medlock.

65
00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:20.627
"You stay where you're told to stay, or you'll find yourself locked up.

66
00:05:20.627 --> 00:05:24.133
Mr. Craven had better get you a governess.

67
00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:29.249
You need someone sharp to look after you. I've got enough to do."

68
00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:34.203
Mrs. Medlock stormed from the room and slammed the door after her.

69
00:05:34.203 --> 00:05:37.451
Mary sat on the rug before the fire.

70
00:05:37.451 --> 00:05:41.822
She did not cry, but she was pale with rage.

71
00:05:41.822 --> 00:05:50.706
"There was someone crying," she said to herself. "There was."
